1
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:13,600
This is part three of the three-part series discussing approach to interpretation of abdominal and pelvic CTs.

2
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:19,440
Given that this is the final module in the series, following completion,

3
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:27,900
you should be able to employ a comprehensive and systematic search pattern for interpreting abdominal and pelvic CTs.

4
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:34,780
Specifically for this module, you should be able to identify abnormalities in blood vessels, lymph nodes,

5
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:39,780
the pelvic organs of both the male and female, the body wall, and the bones.

6
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:47,400
Just to review our overall search pattern, we began with discussion of the abdominal organs,

7
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:52,100
followed by the bowel and mesentery, which we considered a single unit,

8
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:55,480
and then the peritoneum and retroperitoneum.

9
00:00:56,320 --> 00:01:01,820
In this module, we will discuss evaluation of the vessels and lymph nodes, the pelvic organs,

10
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:04,400
and then finally the body wall and bones.

11
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:12,520
Please take a minute to read this sample dictation describing normal findings

12
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:17,040
as they relate to the vessels, lymph nodes, pelvis, body wall, and bones.

13
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:23,580
Assessment of the vessels will include evaluation of both the arteries and veins,

14
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:30,400
and for a standard CT abdomen and pelvis, this will be an assessment of the major abdominal vasculature.

15
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:35,020
On the arterial side, this includes assessment of the abdominal aorta,

16
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:40,220
the mesenteric vessels, that is the celiac artery, superior mesenteric artery,

17
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:45,700
and inferior mesenteric artery, as well as the renal arteries and iliac arteries.

18
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:53,920
You will follow the aorta and its branches from cranial to caudal or from caudal to cranial,

19
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:03,380
but along the length of the vessels and assess for patency, size, and the presence or absence for dissection.

20
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:09,900
When evaluating the arteries, you want to assess the overall caliber, that is,

21
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:14,920
are they too small or stenotic, or are they too large or aneurysmal?

22
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:22,440
The inferrenal abdominal aorta is the most common site for intra-abdominal aortic aneurysms.

23
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:29,160
Aneurysmal dilatation of the inferrenal aorta is defined as a diameter of 3 centimeters,

24
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:39,260
or stated another way, 1.5 times the normal diameter, which in this case, the normal diameter is about 2 centimeters.

25
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:47,920
Aneurysms involving the common and internal iliac arteries are more common than those of the external iliac arteries.

26
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:56,680
Common iliac artery aneurysms are defined with a diameter greater than 2.5 centimeters.

27
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:05,640
Here we can see on this coronal image dilatation of the inferrenal abdominal aorta,

28
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:14,280
as well as both common iliac arteries, and that's confirmed on the axial image.

29
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:23,200
You can see that not all of the lumen is patent, only that central portion that is enhancing is actually patent.

30
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:34,500
On the image on the right, we see bilateral common iliac artery aneurysms, again, with only partially patent lumens.

31
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:38,960
Here are some examples of aortic dissection.

32
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:46,780
Remember that a dissection occurs when blood dissects into the vessel wall, creating a flap.

33
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:50,900
So the flap contains the intima and some media.

34
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:57,000
The other side contains the rest of the media and adventitia.

35
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:03,800
And in doing so, that dissection creates a true lumen, where blood should be, and then the false lumen.

36
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:10,900
The image on the left shows the intima and medial flap with enhancing true and false lumens.

37
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:15,620
The image on the right shows an aortic dissection.

38
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:23,140
We have the enhancing true lumen, and in this case, the false lumen is thrombosed.

39
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:35,180
The major abdominal veins include the portal system veins, including the portal veins, superior mesenteric vein, and splenic veins.

40
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:40,320
The inferior vena cava, hepatic veins, and iliac veins.

41
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:42,860
And then finally, the renal veins.

42
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:49,400
Assessment on the venous side is primarily for patency in the presence or absence of thrombus,

43
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:54,760
although you can occasionally see venous side aneurysms.

44
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:58,820
Here is an example of a thrombus.

45
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:02,780
In this case, the thrombus is only partially occlusive.

46
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:09,940
The image on the left is a coronal view showing a partially occlusive thrombus in the portal vein.

47
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:18,880
You can see how this manifests itself as a filling defect that is visible because of the presence of intravenous contrast material.

48
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:27,580
On the axial image, we can again see this filling defect in the portal vein consistent with a non-occlusive thrombus.

49
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:39,820
As you evaluate the vasculature, you also want to be evaluating the lymph nodes because the lymph nodes live along the vessels.

50
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:47,800
When assessing lymph nodes, you need to evaluate their size, the number of lymph nodes,

51
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:56,840
and their specific imaging appearance in terms of their morphology and shape, as well as their attenuation and enhancement.

52
00:05:59,970 --> 00:06:08,730
A full discussion of all of the size criteria for the lymph node stations of the abdomen is beyond the scope of this lecture.

53
00:06:08,730 --> 00:06:19,910
But as a general rule, think of lymph nodes as being enlarged in the abdomen and pelvis when their short axis is more than 10 millimeters.

54
00:06:20,610 --> 00:06:23,410
And then also realize that there are exceptions.

55
00:06:23,630 --> 00:06:28,750
The most notable exceptions are in the retrochoral or mesorectal space.

56
00:06:28,750 --> 00:06:37,390
Those lymph nodes normally aren't anywhere close to being 10 millimeters in size,

57
00:06:37,510 --> 00:06:42,750
so anything more than 5 millimeters is considered at least potentially enlarged.

58
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:49,610
On the other end of the spectrum, lymph nodes in the portocaval space and the inguinal regions are typically larger,

59
00:06:49,610 --> 00:06:57,690
and their upper limit of normal caliber in the short axis is taken as 15 millimeters.

60
00:07:01,710 --> 00:07:08,650
In terms of imaging appearance other than size, you want to assess for morphology and attenuation.

61
00:07:10,210 --> 00:07:18,590
Abnormal morphologic characteristics of lymph nodes include a round lymph node with loss of a fatty hilum,

62
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:22,870
the central fatty hilum, and the irregular or speculated margins.

63
00:07:22,950 --> 00:07:32,870
Remember that normal lymph nodes are kind of reniform in shape with a central fatty hilum and smooth margins.

64
00:07:33,370 --> 00:07:42,590
In terms of attenuation and enhancement, lymph nodes tend to show uniform, mild enhancement.

65
00:07:42,590 --> 00:07:51,470
Heterogeneous enhancement, whether it be areas of low attenuation from cystic changes or necrosis, is abnormal.

66
00:07:52,210 --> 00:08:00,910
Sometimes lymph nodes can be calcified, and sometimes lymph nodes can actually enhance more than typical or be hyper-enhancing.

67
00:08:03,380 --> 00:08:06,380
Just a reminder of the normal lymph node morphology.

68
00:08:06,380 --> 00:08:16,280
Remember that a normal lymph node is reniform or oval in shape with a peripheral cortex and a central fatty hilum.

69
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:28,360
On the coronal CT on the left in the inguinal region, we can see the fatty hilum and the mildly enhancing peripheral cortex of a normal lymph node.

70
00:08:28,580 --> 00:08:33,500
That same morphology can be appreciated on the axial image in the center.

71
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:49,380
And then ultrasound can nicely show this anatomy, showing the peripheral cortex with the white arrow and the central hilum, which is fat, which is echogenic on ultrasound.

72
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:56,340
And you can see that the main blood supply to the lymph node actually comes through that fatty hilum.

73
00:08:56,340 --> 00:09:03,380
Here are some examples of lymph nodes, which are abnormal in attenuation.

74
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:11,800
On the image on the left, we have a nodal mass with peripheral calcification.

75
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:25,840
The image on the right shows a mesenteric mass, which is enhancing in a heterogeneous fashion with areas of low attenuation, which were consistent with necrosis.

76
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:39,540
This question tests your knowledge of size criteria for aneurysms within the abdomen and pelvis.

77
00:09:40,340 --> 00:09:53,560
The most common site to get an abdominal aortic aneurysm is in its infrarenal segment, that is below the level of the renal arteries, between the renal arteries and the aortic bifurcation.

78
00:09:53,560 --> 00:10:01,560
If this segment of the aorta measures three centimeters or more, that is considered an aneurysm.

79
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:09,220
Following evaluation of the vessels and lymph nodes, we can then evaluate the organs of the pelvis.

80
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:15,660
The common organ, as comparing males and females, will be the urinary bladder, so we'll start with this.

81
00:10:15,660 --> 00:10:21,920
In terms of the bladder, you want to assess the overall distension at the time of imaging.

82
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:26,380
The wall thickness, which will vary depending on the degree of distension.

83
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:31,360
In other words, a more distended bladder will tend to thin out the wall.

84
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:41,640
And then finally, you want to evaluate the lumen in terms of the overall attenuation, as well as looking for masses or stones.

85
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:49,640
You need to evaluate the bladder wall for diffuse or focal thickening.

86
00:10:50,860 --> 00:11:00,260
When doing so, you need to take into account the degree of bladder distension, remembering, similar to the bowel that we discussed,

87
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:09,600
the wall thickness of this viscous will vary depending on the degree of distension.

88
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:16,540
So an under-distended bladder may cause apparent wall thickening, which doesn't represent true pathologic wall thickening.

89
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:22,260
The image on the left shows circumferential bladder wall thickening.

90
00:11:22,260 --> 00:11:31,720
The image on the right shows outpouchings from the primary bladder lumen that are filled with fluid attenuation material

91
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:35,160
and contiguous with that bladder lumen through these openings.

92
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:38,720
This is consistent with bladder diverticula.

93
00:11:41,420 --> 00:11:46,700
Here are some examples of abnormal attenuation within the urinary bladder.

94
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:55,180
The image on the left shows high attenuation in the dependent portion of the bladder, which turned out to be a large blood clot.

95
00:11:56,540 --> 00:12:06,620
The image in the middle shows layering high attenuation material within the bladder, which represented excreted contrast material.

96
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:16,660
And then finally, the image on the right shows mass-like thickening in the non-dependent portion of the urinary bladder,

97
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:19,100
which represented a urothelial carcinoma.

98
00:12:21,530 --> 00:12:24,750
Here are additional examples of the bladder mass.

99
00:12:25,110 --> 00:12:30,150
The coronal image on the left shows a contrast-filled urinary bladder.

100
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:41,550
The bladder mass, as shown here, is identified as a filling defect within that contrast column within the urinary bladder.

101
00:12:42,030 --> 00:12:49,650
The image on the right shows a faintly visible bladder mass with subtle calcifications along its ventral margin.

102
00:12:51,270 --> 00:12:58,050
Finally, when assessing the bladder lumen, you want to evaluate for bladder calculi.

103
00:12:58,050 --> 00:13:03,630
And the key, when you identify a calculus which appears to be within the bladder,

104
00:13:04,010 --> 00:13:12,590
is to actually distinguish a true bladder calculus from a calculus that is impacted at the ureovesical junction.

105
00:13:13,750 --> 00:13:15,530
The key here is location.

106
00:13:16,210 --> 00:13:20,370
Bladder calculi are typically located in the most dependent portion of the bladder.

107
00:13:21,190 --> 00:13:23,270
So let's take two different examples here.

108
00:13:23,270 --> 00:13:32,570
The image on the left shows no calculus in the dependent, or most dependent, portion of the bladder.

109
00:13:33,110 --> 00:13:36,070
Instead, the calcification is to the right of the midline.

110
00:13:36,890 --> 00:13:43,390
And this is actually a calculus that's located at the right ureovesical junction.

111
00:13:44,450 --> 00:13:50,090
Contrast that to the image on the right, which shows two large calculi.

112
00:13:50,090 --> 00:13:59,290
These are positioned in the most dependent portion of the bladder, which is dorsal or posterior, in a supine patient.

113
00:14:01,820 --> 00:14:10,500
In the female patient, you will need to evaluate the uterus to include the cervix as well as the vagina and ovaries.

114
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:18,100
Uterine evaluation will include assessment of the myometrium and endometrium.

115
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:26,740
The endometrium, myomyomas, or fibroids, are the most common pathology within the myometrium.

116
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:35,680
The image on the left shows hypo-enhancing fibroids surrounded by normally enhancing uterine myometrium.

117
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:46,100
The image in the middle and the image on the right show a calcified fibroid arising from the uterine fundus.

118
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:54,170
The endometrium is typically evaluated with pelvic ultrasound or MRI.

119
00:14:54,730 --> 00:14:59,150
However, it is still something that you need to evaluate on CT.

120
00:14:59,510 --> 00:15:05,450
Sagittal CT is especially useful for assessment of the uterus and endometrium.

121
00:15:06,150 --> 00:15:11,510
Here we have two axial images showing fairly obvious abnormalities,

122
00:15:11,510 --> 00:15:17,550
which can be localized to the endometrium, both of which represent endometrial cancer.

123
00:15:18,110 --> 00:15:30,810
In the image on the left, we see a markedly distended endometrial cavity containing a soft tissue attenuation mass.

124
00:15:30,810 --> 00:15:39,910
The image on the right shows an endometrial mass, and again, both of these represent endometrial carcinoma.

125
00:15:40,010 --> 00:15:45,990
But the point here is for the uterus, you want to assess both the myometrium and the endometrium.

126
00:15:46,070 --> 00:15:53,470
And when you're evaluating endometrium, you want to look for overall thickness as well as for the presence of a mass.

127
00:15:53,470 --> 00:16:03,330
When evaluating the ovaries, you want to look for ovarian size and position as well as for focal ovarian masses.

128
00:16:04,750 --> 00:16:12,870
CT is limited for evaluation of the ovaries in most cases, although as we will see later,

129
00:16:14,310 --> 00:16:19,610
ovarian teratomas that contain fat or calcium are typically easily identified on CT.

130
00:16:19,610 --> 00:16:35,290
However, overall, CT is limited relative to ultrasound because CT is not really able to characterize that internal architecture of the ovaries

131
00:16:35,290 --> 00:16:38,250
in terms of septations and complexity.

132
00:16:38,810 --> 00:16:47,850
As you can see in this example, where the ovaries look fairly simple on the CT in terms of their internal characteristics,

133
00:16:47,850 --> 00:16:59,010
but on ultrasound, you can identify the characteristic ovarian morphology with multiple follicles in this premenopausal patient.

134
00:17:00,290 --> 00:17:08,070
Here are three separate examples of pelvic masses which are ovarian in origin.

135
00:17:09,170 --> 00:17:12,850
Sometimes the origin of the pelvic mass can be difficult to identify.

136
00:17:12,850 --> 00:17:23,890
One of the keys to identifying that something is from the ovary is to actually follow the gonadal veins from their origin,

137
00:17:24,010 --> 00:17:35,050
either from the IVC or the left renal vein down into the pelvis to see if they connect with the mass in question.

138
00:17:35,050 --> 00:17:42,750
So, if you can follow those gonadal veins into the pelvic mass, that is suggestive of an ovarian origin.

139
00:17:45,210 --> 00:17:51,730
Here are examples of ovarian teratomas, and CT is very good at characterizing teratomas,

140
00:17:51,830 --> 00:17:57,610
particularly if they contain fat because this is highly specific for the teratoma.

141
00:17:57,810 --> 00:18:02,050
Another key finding is the presence of calcium, although this is less specific.

142
00:18:02,050 --> 00:18:09,470
The image on the left shows fat within an ovarian mass consistent with a teratoma.

143
00:18:09,970 --> 00:18:13,350
The image on the right contains both fat and calcium.

144
00:18:13,630 --> 00:18:16,670
The calcium is indicated by the arrow.

145
00:18:17,330 --> 00:18:23,670
And then you can see back on the left-sided image that the teratoma also contains soft tissue elements.

146
00:18:23,670 --> 00:18:34,470
So, these can contain elements from all three germ cell layers and can have a very complex appearance on CT and MR.

147
00:18:34,590 --> 00:18:42,030
The key really is the fat and calcium, and especially the fat within the mass.

148
00:18:43,650 --> 00:18:50,230
Moving on to the male patient, you need to assess the prostate and seminal vesicles.

149
00:18:50,230 --> 00:18:59,030
The example on the left shows a midline cystic mass in the prostate, consistent with a congenital utricle cyst.

150
00:19:00,410 --> 00:19:10,410
The image in the center and the image on the right are from the same patient that shows an enlarged prostate with median lobe hypertrophy,

151
00:19:11,630 --> 00:19:18,730
exerting mass effect on the adjacent bladder, and can cause, as it does in this case, some bladder outlet obstruction.

152
00:19:18,730 --> 00:19:26,130
So, when you're assessing the prostate, you want to look at overall size, evaluate if there's any mass effect on the bladder base

153
00:19:26,130 --> 00:19:31,530
that might be a cause of outlet obstruction, and then to look for focal masses.

154
00:19:33,150 --> 00:19:36,390
For the seminal vesicles, you want to make sure that both are present,

155
00:19:36,590 --> 00:19:40,730
and you want to evaluate for underlying masses or congenital cysts.

156
00:19:40,730 --> 00:19:51,530
The image on the left shows a normal right seminal vesicle with an absent left seminal vesicle.

157
00:19:52,530 --> 00:20:01,130
Absent seminal vesicles or seminal vesicle cysts are clinically important because they're often associated with underlying renal abnormalities,

158
00:20:01,130 --> 00:20:04,130
including an absent ipsilateral kidney.

159
00:20:04,130 --> 00:20:11,130
So, the kidney can be absent on the side of the seminal vesicle cyst or the absent seminal vesicle.

160
00:20:12,730 --> 00:20:21,130
Calcified vas deferens, as shown on the image on the right, are typically indicative of patients with diabetes mellitus.

161
00:20:21,130 --> 00:20:36,380
Lymph nodes can be abnormal for multiple reasons, including being too large in size, too conspicuous in number,

162
00:20:37,020 --> 00:20:41,760
or have some morphologic or attenuation abnormality.

163
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:49,740
Of the imaging findings listed here, loss of the fatty hilum was the pathologic finding.

164
00:20:50,460 --> 00:20:55,840
Remember that a normal lymph node should have a preserved central fatty hilum,

165
00:20:55,960 --> 00:20:59,600
and loss of that fatty hilum can be indicative of pathology.

166
00:21:01,300 --> 00:21:10,880
Evaluation of the body wall and the bones will round out our comprehensive systematic search pattern

167
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:14,020
when interpreting abdominal and pelvic CTs.

168
00:21:14,520 --> 00:21:23,840
Assessment of the body wall will include evaluation for body wall edema, masses, and hernias.

169
00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:31,980
Ventral hernias are the most common types of abdominal wall hernias that you will encounter.

170
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:36,260
These occur when there's a defect in the linea alba.

171
00:21:36,260 --> 00:21:46,800
As identified on the image on the left, we can see this large mesenteric fat and small bowel containing midline ventral hernia.

172
00:21:47,160 --> 00:21:53,520
You can also have a defect at the linea semilunaris, that is, at the junction of the rectus abdominis

173
00:21:53,520 --> 00:21:59,140
and the anterolateral muscles of the abdominal wall.

174
00:21:59,800 --> 00:22:02,360
These are termed spigelian hernias.

175
00:22:02,360 --> 00:22:10,300
The image on the right shows a left-sided spigelian hernia.

176
00:22:11,220 --> 00:22:18,240
And for comparison, on the normal side, you can see an intact linea semilunaris.

177
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:26,400
When evaluating for inguinal hernias, the key landmark will be the inferior epigastric vessels.

178
00:22:26,780 --> 00:22:32,560
This will help classify inguinal hernias as either direct or indirect.

179
00:22:32,560 --> 00:22:38,100
The internal epigastric vessels are marked on this axial image.

180
00:22:41,510 --> 00:22:51,290
The key to characterize inguinal hernias will be to identify the neck of the hernia as it relates to the inferior epigastric vessels.

181
00:22:51,290 --> 00:22:59,090
The neck of an indirect hernia will originate lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels as shown here.

182
00:23:00,370 --> 00:23:12,130
On the other hand, the neck of a direct inguinal hernia will originate medial to the inferior epigastric vessels as shown on the right-sided image.

183
00:23:12,130 --> 00:23:24,350
Here is an example of a body wall abnormality consisting of multiple soft tissue masses, which represented melanoma metastasis.

184
00:23:24,350 --> 00:23:29,670
We will end with assessment of the bones.

185
00:23:30,170 --> 00:23:37,850
We will need to evaluate the spine, pelvis, proximal femurs, and ribs, as well as the sacroiliac and hip joints.

186
00:23:37,850 --> 00:23:49,310
You need to first make an overall assessment of bone density so that you don't miss a global abnormality such as diffuse osteopenia or sclerosis.

187
00:23:49,810 --> 00:23:59,370
Then you can start to look for focal lesions such as metastasis or benign bone lesions or fractures as they relate to trauma.

188
00:23:59,370 --> 00:24:04,450
And then finally, you can assess for degenerative changes.

189
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:12,900
This is an example of diffuse bone disease in a patient with renal osteodystrophy.

190
00:24:13,080 --> 00:24:21,280
There is diffuse abnormal sclerosis of the bone, and this can sometimes be difficult to appreciate given its diffuse nature.

191
00:24:25,140 --> 00:24:29,520
Focal bone lesions can be either osteoblastic or bone forming.

192
00:24:29,520 --> 00:24:39,840
So these are sclerotic lesions as shown on the image on the left, or they can be lucent or bone destroying.

193
00:24:40,680 --> 00:24:46,380
And these are termed osteolytic lesions as demonstrated on the image on the right.

194
00:24:49,220 --> 00:24:51,700
Another focal abnormality would be a fracture.

195
00:24:52,400 --> 00:24:58,600
Here we see several examples of fractures within the spine and pelvis.

196
00:24:58,600 --> 00:25:03,620
Pelvis and fractures are typically fairly straightforward in their imaging appearance.

197
00:25:03,780 --> 00:25:10,860
There will be an area of low attenuation or a crack or break within the bone, plus or minus displacement.

198
00:25:11,420 --> 00:25:19,360
In summary, in this module we've discussed evaluation of the vessels and lymph nodes,

199
00:25:20,260 --> 00:25:25,820
the organs of the pelvis in both the male and female, and then finally the body wall and bones.

200
00:25:25,820 --> 00:25:33,420
In terms of the vessels, we need to identify the major abdominal arteries and veins.

201
00:25:34,160 --> 00:25:40,680
We should start at the cranial aspect of the CT and head caudally,

202
00:25:40,920 --> 00:25:46,820
looking at the abdominal aorta and its mesenteric renal and iliac branches.

203
00:25:46,820 --> 00:25:52,700
On the venous side, we need to evaluate the portal venous system, particularly the portal vein,

204
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:57,420
as well as the hepatic veins, inferior vena cava and iliac veins.

205
00:25:58,160 --> 00:26:06,100
For vessels, we're primarily looking for abnormalities in size and patency.

206
00:26:06,100 --> 00:26:16,360
As we evaluate the vessels, we can also assess for the lymph nodes, as these tend to follow the vessels.

207
00:26:16,840 --> 00:26:25,680
When we assess the lymph nodes, we need to be evaluating size, number, morphology, and attenuation and enhancement.

208
00:26:25,680 --> 00:26:35,680
In terms of the pelvis, we need to assess the urinary bladder for overall wall thickness and any intraluminal abnormalities,

209
00:26:36,480 --> 00:26:40,480
such as stones, masses, blood clots, etc.

210
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:49,740
In the female pelvis, we need to evaluate the uterus, both in terms of the myometrium and endometrium, as well as the ovaries.

211
00:26:49,740 --> 00:26:55,960
In the male, we need to assess the prostate, including its mass effect on the bladder,

212
00:26:56,740 --> 00:27:00,160
and we need to evaluate the seminal vesicles.

213
00:27:00,840 --> 00:27:07,460
We end our comprehensive evaluation with assessment of the body wall and bones.

214
00:27:07,740 --> 00:27:13,020
The body wall, we want to look for edema, hernias, masses, and fluid collections.

215
00:27:13,020 --> 00:27:20,160
And in the bones, we are assessing not only for focal abnormalities, such as fractures or tumors,

216
00:27:20,360 --> 00:27:27,360
but also for diffuse abnormalities, because we don't want to miss a diffuse finding within the bones,

217
00:27:27,460 --> 00:27:31,000
such as osteopenia or diffuse sclerosis.

218
00:27:32,300 --> 00:27:34,500
That brings us to the end of this unit.

219
00:27:35,020 --> 00:27:39,760
Please complete the corresponding quiz questions to receive credit for this course.

